      "name": "broccoli",
      "username": "broccoli",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/broccoli/{size}/1861_2.png",
      "created_at": "2014-02-07T14:41:34.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Veet\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/v/f05b48/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Veet:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I actually kind of figuired the H scaling effect might be balanced out by the fact that they wouldn't have their secondary effect go off every turn like the others.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>The problem is though that it results in double scaling. &nbsp;Playing against the V5 already scales, because you're playing against (H) V5 decks. &nbsp;If one of the members has damage that scales with (H), then that individual member becomes tougher or easier, in addition to having more partners. &nbsp;Now, if you want to make the Ennead member harder, you can fix (H) as higher (always 4, always 5), but you really don't want it to actually fluctuate with (H).</p>",
      "post_number": 9,
